Kerberos Authentication in Active Directory

Date: 10 October 2026
Platform / Environment: Windows — Active Directory and IT Support fundamentals

What I Learned

* Kerberos is a key authentication protocol used by Active Directory environments.
* A Ticket-Granting Ticket (TGT) allows a client to request service tickets without repeating the entire initial authentication process for each service.
* A service ticket is used when authenticating to a particular service.
* Authentication verifies identity, while authorisation determines what that identity is allowed to access.
* Incorrect time synchronisation can cause Kerberos authentication failures.

Key Takeaways

* Successful authentication does not guarantee permission to access a resource.
* klist can display and help investigate cached Kerberos tickets.
* w32tm can help investigate Windows Time synchronisation.
* When troubleshooting access problems, distinguish authentication failures from permission-related issues.

Practical Relevance

These concepts help explain how domain-joined Windows computers authenticate users and access network services. They also provide a foundation for troubleshooting access problems before moving on to NTFS and shared-folder permissions.
